  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.2 x^2 + 0.6 z^2) / 2, drawn at half its value as height. x (across the screen) is steep. Plain steps multiply x by 1 - 2.2 × rate, so swings grow for rates above about 0.91.",
    "Each step = -rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian on both axes with spread 1.5 × sqrt((64 - b) / (b × 63)), so it is exactly zero at b = 64. Noise values come from the fixed seed, numbered by run and step.",
    "Both arrows are steps (rate × slope), not the slope itself, drawn at 4 times their true length on one shared scale. The purple one is the step the ball takes next. The dark one is the step using all 64 points.",
    "Diverged: the loss goes past 4.4, the loss at the bowl's steep edge. Finished: 40 steps. Points off the bowl are drawn at its rim. The Loss readout always shows the true value.",
    "The trail holds the start point plus up to 40 landing points, one per step. The ghost keeps the last run that took at least one step. Running mode takes 5 steps a second."
  ]
}